\chapter{Writing a Package.mull}
A package is ordinary MuLL source evaluated in its own environment. Its
top-level bindings do not become application globals. It receives \api{G}
and returns a record containing its public exports.

\section{A script package}
Place the following in \api{your-root/counter/Package.mull}:
\begin{code}
var n <- 0;
return {
    Next: fn(){ n <- n + 1; return n; },
    Reset: fn(){ n <- 0; }
};
\end{code}
After adding \api{your-root} to the runtime's paths, import \api{counter} and
call \api{Counter:Next()}. Both exported functions capture the package's
private cell. Repeated imports share that cell through the package cache.
No dedicated export statement is necessary.

\section{A native package}
The supplied libraries use a small entry point:
\begin{code}
return G:Native("printf");
\end{code}
The corresponding \api{Native.hpp} or \api{Native.d} registers the record
with the host runtime. A package can wrap that record, add script helpers,
or combine several imported records into a new interface. Native code is
compiled with the embedding application; importing does not invoke a compiler.

\section{Initialization effects}
Initialization runs once per successful import. If it raises an error, the
cache is not populated, but host effects already performed are not undone.
Keep initialization small and make retry behavior deliberate. A package that
needs another package should use the same \api{LoadLib(... ) or DieWith(...)}
idiom as application code so dependency failures remain informative.
